    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>The <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-wallpaper-resolution.json')}>wallpaper preparation report</a> records native physical-size and integer-scale changes, rapid resize bursts, preserved source/preferences and the unchanged desktop process. <code>BackgroundTarget</code> validates checked physical dimensions in the presentation layer. The child retains one accepted refit and the latest size; the injected asset adapter prepares pixels. Framework image storage preserves natural aspect independently of retained mip dimensions. Last-good pixels remain visible after a normal decoding failure, with explicit retry. Decoder scratch, transient old/new images, fullscreen widget/open-overlay buffers and lock/login preparation still need separate work.</p>
    <p>The earlier <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-dock-surface.json')}>compact-dock milestone</a> measured <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-dock-surface-before-120.json')}>284.22 MiB for the prior binary</a> and <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-dock-surface-after-120.json')}>254.10 MiB for the new binary</a>, an observed 30.12 MiB reduction. This fresh comparison uses the same wallpaper, prepared-asset payloads and detected display/workload metadata. The new release was sampled first, then the baseline; both ran for 120 seconds without profiling on a shared host. The earlier 293.25 MiB sample had a slightly different wallpaper payload and is not the baseline for this change. This sample is within the current {performanceTargets.normal_desktop_rss_mib} MiB RSS target. No CPU, startup or animation improvement is claimed.</p>
    <p>The settled native dock is 1920 × 120 logical pixels at 1080p/1×, keeping its original display-grid position and whole shadow. One BGRA8 buffer payload is 0.88 MiB instead of 7.91 MiB; buffer counts, padding and total driver memory are unknown. The launcher child waits for a full submission before opening and a settled closing submission before shrinking. Sixteen paired compact/full comparisons pass on each Vulkan driver, and all 212 existing snapshots retain their baselines. Native pointer/keyboard, mode cycling, outside close, interrupted reopen, terminal launch, integer scaling and zero closed-dock idle frames pass, along with the existing desktop workflow.</p>
    <p>The <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-widget-surfaces.json')}>bounded-widget milestone</a> moves settled cards into smaller native buffers. Overlapping paint bounds share a buffer so their original drawing order survives; disjoint groups do not rely on ordering within a Wayland layer. During capture, source geometry stays fixed and retains input without painting, while a full-display pointer-pass-through preview shows the cards and grid. Twenty-four paired scenarios pass on both available Vulkan drivers, including overlapping cards, narrow displays, both palettes and integer scales 1 and 2. Native placement, capture, snapped release, restart persistence and integer-scale cancellation pass, along with the existing desktop workflow.</p>
    <p>The fresh, matching 120-second comparison measured <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-widget-surfaces-before-120.json')}>252.18 MiB for the previous release</a> and <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-widget-surfaces-after-120.json')}>224.93 MiB for the new release</a>, an observed 27.25 MiB reduction. The new release ran first, then the baseline under the same supervisor, wallpaper and workload; detected metadata and prepared assets match. The three settled widget buffers total 1.94 MiB of one-buffer BGRA8 payload, versus 7.91 MiB for the previous full-output buffer. Buffer counts, padding and driver allocations are unknown. This is a shared-host software-Vulkan comparison, with no CPU, startup or animation improvement claim; this sample is within the current {performanceTargets.normal_desktop_rss_mib} MiB RSS target.</p>
    <p>Native tests exposed the old supervisor waiting for a permanent <code>widgets</code> buffer and stopping the new desktop after 30 seconds. Supervision and activation now share a pure readiness contract requiring first submissions from the declared widget groups; all-hidden widgets and the previous fullscreen client are supported. Both measured binaries stayed alive for their full samples. Test cleanup also checkpoints owned wallpaper snapshots and restores through IPC, verifying the active link instead of overwriting it with a stale legacy target.</p>
    <p>Open overlays and the active drag preview retain full-output buffers. Decoding peaks, allocator/driver retention, per-output/fractional geometry and hardware frame pacing remain separate work. One observed dock opening submission gap reached 33.69 ms in the software-Vulkan sample; this does not establish smooth scanout or a flicker-free hardware transition.</p>
    <p>Earlier <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-preview-cache-before-120.json')}>588.26 MiB</a> and <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-preview-cache-after-120.json')}>389.93 MiB</a> runs established the observed lazy-preview improvement on an earlier binary. The <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-preview-cache.json')}>native cache test</a> browsed a generated 73-image library, reached beyond the old preload limit, recovered a corrupt preview through retry, and verified the smaller closed-browser budget. Both live providers supplied thumbnails without changing the wallpaper or palette. These measurements do not establish hardware frame pacing or total GPU memory.</p>
    <p>The <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-icon-masks.json')}>monochrome coverage and allocation milestone</a> stores themed icon and text coverage in one channel and requests wgpu's supported memory allocation preference. Dimensions, text supersampling, mip filtering and existing visual references stay the same. Prepared icon payload falls from 11.83 to 2.96 MiB; colored icons and wallpapers retain the RGBA path.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Same VM boot, 120-second sample</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Mean process RSS</td><td>226.78 MiB</td><td>207.70 MiB</td><td>Observed reduction: 19.08 MiB, about 8.4%. This historical sample is within the current {performanceTargets.normal_desktop_rss_mib} MiB RSS target; the rebuilt Bluetooth sample is reported above.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Maximum sampled RSS</td><td>229.29 MiB</td><td>211.08 MiB</td><td>Warm single-output software-Vulkan workload; active overlays and decoder peaks need separate work.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Main-process CPU, one core</td><td>0.767%</td><td>0.767%</td><td>No CPU or hardware frame-pacing improvement is claimed.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Idle background / closed-dock frames</td><td>0 / 0</td><td>0 / 0</td><td>Event-driven rendering retained; visible widgets still update their data.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>The <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-icon-masks-before-120.json')}>baseline</a> and <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-icon-masks-after-120.json')}>new release</a> ran sequentially in the same capped VM boot with matching display, workload and background metadata. The current release was restored and its running hash verified after the baseline. Earlier observations remain in the report: masks alone did not show lower average RSS across separate boots, then a same-boot run measured a smaller reduction. These payload counters exclude driver allocations; measured RSS includes mapped driver/software-GPU pages. Actual GPU memory, hardware pacing, long idle and fault behavior remain unverified.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Implication</th><th>Next test / owner</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Prepared images, active GPU textures, caches and surface buffers have different lifetimes.</td><td>The unused texture-cache budget does not cap process RSS. Active scene resources remain pinned, and driver allocations are outside the reported payload counters.</td><td>Measure app asset ownership alongside renderer payloads and Linux mappings. Asset policy belongs in presentation adapters; GPU retention belongs in the renderer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>NativeAssets previously retained a 4096-pixel background at every display size.</td><td>Resize events supply logical size and integer scale; worker preparation retains sufficient cover mips for both physical edges. Source decoding still uses the original limits, and discarded GPU textures can remain in the reuse cache.</td><td>Native size/scale changes and bounded stale-result/retry tests pass. Four focused image/clipping checks pass on each of software/hardware Vulkan, and 212 desktop comparisons retain their existing baselines/tolerances. Framebuffer and peak-decoder costs remain open.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Selected wallpaper previews previously accumulated in Desktop.images without eviction.</td><td>A presentation coordinator now bounds local preview demand/entries, evicts unwanted images and reduces reuse after closing. Application-owned prepared storage has deduplicated byte accounting; in-flight work and other owners are excluded.</td><td>Rapid-navigation, retry, stale-result/transaction precedence, bounded retention and accounting tests passed, along with native browsing/retry checks. The paired RSS reports show the observed process change; disk snapshot retention and GPU cache lifetimes remain separate.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A profiled cold launcher opening had a 101.35 ms submission gap; its largest recorded dock frame cost was 10.63 ms.</td><td>Successful dock-frame work alone does not explain the gap. A later same-binary normal run had a 24.25 ms cold maximum; that does not prove the earlier hitch fixed.</td><td>Correlate input, update, reconciliation, other surfaces and submission timestamps. Keep profiling in runtime/renderer, with bounded storage and no idle redraws.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Text rasterization still runs synchronously on a cache miss.</td><td>Warm text can be cheap while a new query, font or scale incurs preparation work. Cache-hit behavior cannot establish first-use responsiveness.</td><td>Measure realistic large application lists and font/scale transitions before choosing asynchronous text preparation or prewarming.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hardware and software Vulkan pass the existing visual suite after explicit fine-derivative coverage.</td><td>This establishes selected pixel correctness within tolerance, not native hardware frame pacing, power use or GPU execution time.</td><td>Run the actual desktop on hardware and test fractional scaling, hotplug and sleep/resume separately.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>The <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-frame-profile-120.json')}>profiled report</a> stores bounded CPU wall times. Text rasterization and texture upload are nested inside preparation; adding those numbers would double-count work. Submission/presentation may include driver waits. They are not Vulkan GPU timestamps or monitor scanout measurements.</p>
