  <section id="gaps"><span class="eyebrow">06 / IMPLEMENTATION COVERAGE</span><h2>A working shell.<br />An incomplete desktop platform.</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Current limit</th><th>Required boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Control center and tray</td><td>Audio routing, personal Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, power/profile/brightness and StatusNotifier/DBusMenu have native SDK panels and independent service consumers. Enterprise credential flows, broader Bluetooth/profile acceptance, native power hardware and broader tray application interoperability remain open; owned SNI/DBusMenu fixtures already pass native VM input and lifecycle checks. The later genuine wired NetworkManager Applet run passes five native bar/menu/information-action and four cleanup checks on unchanged <code>8e</code>, one virtual output; thirteen fresh outside-worker checks verify package removal and user-state preservation.</td><td>Typed domain snapshots and commands, explicit ports and platform lifecycle ownership are implemented. The <a href={sourceUrl('docs/native-power.md')}>power</a> and <a href={sourceUrl('docs/native-tray.md')}>tray</a> guides distinguish owned protocol checks from remaining device/application acceptance.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Text input and shaping</td><td>Shared caret/selection and grapheme/word editing serve ordinary desktop fields, including media import. Clipboard uses bounded native pipes and current-field tickets. Multiline Notes editing, compositor clipboard exchange with wl-copy/wl-paste and restart persistence pass 17 functional and nine restoration checks on one output. Merged Text <code>8e6d074b</code> implements script/bidi shaping, bounded fallback and ordinary IME composition, retaining hard-paragraph levels across wraps with exact row-bound context and cache guards. All six CI checks pass, including the full workspace, controlled protocols and 832 visual cases. The fresh owned native IME suite passes five feature and five restoration checks. Third-party/physical/cross-output IME, broader native pixel/scroll details and delayed/broader GUI clipboard acceptance remain open. Separately, the later <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-native-gui-clipboard-2044.json')}>real Chromium/Notes keyboard transfer suite</a> passes nine interaction and twelve restoration checks in both directions on unchanged <code>8e</code>, one virtual output. This does not establish rich content, other apps, delayed transfers, physical hardware or IME behavior. Formal accessibility semantics and platform bridging are deferred by the user.</td><td>Current client state owns value/selection and bounded undo/redo history. Typed edits and shared hit/paint geometry have behavior checks; current undo/redo and native acceptance belong to the <a href={sourceUrl('docs/text-editing.md')}>text guide</a> and ledger.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Displays and widgets</td><td>Named outputs have independent bars/backgrounds/widget hosts, persisted layouts and typed surface instances. Widget resizing and optional fractional density are implemented. A later 16-check native run on unchanged <code>8e</code> passes placement, real panel input, density at 1×/2×/1.25×, removal/recreation and exact restoration across two simultaneous outputs, one owned headless. Cross-output clipboard, physical displays/cable hotplug, suspend and secure outputs remain unverified; richer widget reflow remains open. Native output arrangement has a draft/Apply/Keep/Revert client, ordered fresh reads and an independent rollback guardian. Seven one-output QEMU input checks pass for mode, scale, position, persistence, explicit Revert, timeout and UI-crash EOF recovery. A later harness <code>86df4e5b</code> on unchanged <code>35ed</code> passes four two-output draft/Apply/Revert/timeout/Keep flows. Its 1280×960 headless peer changes position or scale 1/1.25 while the original 1920×1080 scale-1 output stays unchanged. Five positive owner checks and independent retirement/normal-OFF facts verify original monitor bytes/inode, preferences, supervision, output/workspace/cursor restoration. Monitor ctime/link count are not claimed unchanged. That historical four-flow run kept all input on the original 1× panel. A later harness <code>1774fa87</code> on runtime <code>26d03adf</code> passes nine virtual Display workflows, adding rotation/Revert, disable/Keep, re-enable through actual controls/Keep, fractional scale/Keep and native pointer/keyboard Apply/Revert on the fractional peer panel. Its panel is 1024×768 logical units, density 1.25, with a 1280×960 buffer. Five positive preservation checks, one ctime/link-count scope marker and three cursor checks pass. Independent later normal-OFF facts verify the same desktop lifetime, original output and unchanged 14 source/43 security entries. Physical displays/cable hotplug, suspend/resume, compositor restart, secure-output changes, resource targets and pacing remain open.</td><td>The <a href={sourceUrl('docs/native-displays.md')}>display guide</a> and <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-display-settings.json')}>acceptance report</a> separate strict/pixel checks, actual native input and the explicit post-run correction for the expected supervisor restart. The historical <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-native-two-output-displays.json')}>four-flow Display report</a> keeps its original checks and limitations. The later <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-native-two-output-displays-26d-nine.json')}>nine-flow Display report</a> separates nine feature checks, five positive preservation checks, one timestamp/link-count scope marker, three cursor checks and independent normal-OFF verification. Its 26 native submissions establish prepared geometry, not presentation FPS. The initial wrong-dispatch attempt failed before checks; its immutable failure and separate guarded recovery are retained. Final site, exact-head CI and publication for this follow-up remain pending. The <a href={sourceUrl('docs/monitor-layouts.md')}>monitor guide</a> describes client profile policy; the <a href={sourceUrl('docs/fractional-scaling.md')}>density guide</a> describes SDK/native rendering. Later single-output live-video resize and integer/fractional retargeting pass. The separate <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-native-multi-output.json')}>16-check two-output run</a> establishes its own density/input/lifetime scope; physical/secure-output, cross-output clipboard, suspend and presentation acceptance remain separate.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Launcher and dock</td><td>Live catalog observation, identity-preserving selection, persisted pin/reorder, animated autohide and ordinary desktop-service activation are implemented. Declared actions retain their recorded native scope. Merged Application bb304907 shipped in the accepted-baseline 8e release and implements fresh input-bound activation and explicit file/URI Open. A later real Chromium run with independent consumer d1, now merged through PR89, passes nine feature and eight restoration checks, including observed focus and local Open/action/socket/CLI; other applications, remote URI handlers and direct token consumption remain unverified.</td><td>Application discovery/watch and native activation stay behind the port; launcher/dock behavior belongs to the client. Historical compact-dock pointer/keyboard and launch checks are recorded above; those do not establish native acceptance for the newer catalog/pin/actions/autohide paths.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Live wallpapers</td><td>Local MP4/WebM/GIF import, posters, shared per-output playback, pause, sleep suspension and reusable Vulkan texture updates are implemented. Recorded VM sources pass native import/input/FPS, changing captured pixels, pause/resume, invalid-import preservation, undo/redo, pause-all preservation and restart/restoration. Single-output fullscreen pause, hidden-workspace playback, manual-pause precedence and saved policy controls also pass. Battery/fullscreen policies and shared-output freezing have owned-fixture coverage. Historical image-lifetime 120-second software-Vulkan samples peaked at 247.02 MiB static and 333.39 MiB for the 1280×800 video at 15 FPS. The later Weather-source video sample records 424.12 MiB mean and 424.15 MiB peak RSS; it is a different workload, not an isolated regression comparison. Weak image-storage lifetime observation releases unused textures; the earlier same-workload video peak was 348.88 MiB. Video remains over 300 MiB, and CPU was higher in this one-run comparison. Physical battery transitions, native multi-output behavior, hardware decode and physical-GPU pacing remain open. Actual native secure lock, desktop restart while locked and authenticated unlock/manual-pause preservation pass; Scene/Web projects require additional engines.</td><td><a href={sourceUrl('docs/live-wallpapers.md')}>The media guide</a> separates domain preferences, playback port, FFmpeg adapter, client mailbox/output policy and generic ImageStream. The <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-live-wallpaper-vm.json')}>playback report</a> and <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-live-wallpaper-policy.json')}>policy report</a> identify their tested sources and exact restoration checks. The <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-live-wallpaper-lock.json')}>lock report</a> records independent secure-lock acceptance, and the <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-image-lifetime.json')}>image lifetime report</a> records its historical memory samples and native fade-pixel acceptance. The <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-live-wallpaper-previews.json')}>live-preview report</a> adds native select-only browsing, animated crops, retry/refresh and exact restoration on one 1× output; no preview-specific RSS, scanout or smoothness result is claimed. Multi-output, suspend/reconnect and secure-session display acceptance remain open.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Notifications and service health</td><td>Grouped durable history and DND, inline styles/links, prepared images and current-version actions are implemented. A bounded native storage worker separates archive I/O from live delivery. Canonical <code>UriPort::open(target, context)</code> forwards launch context through the <code>XdgUri</code> adapter. The later <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-default-uri-native-26d.json')}>R9 run</a>, harness <code>563781a2</code> on runtime <code>26d03adf</code>, passes five scoped warm-default-route checks and nine cleanup checks: native pointer and Tab/Enter input each load one selected document with fresh context through a controlled MIME default in an already warm real browser. Independent later normal-OFF facts verify the same desktop lifetime and unchanged 14 source/43 security entries. Activewindow matches do not prove actual browser keyboard focus; automatic history release, browser key receipt, fresh-browser initial focus and direct token consumption remain unverified. Separate client fix <code>194c0c57</code> implements current-session-only history release and passes 372 desktop tests (six ignored) plus strict release Clippy; deployment and actual browser key delivery remain pending. V7’s pre-link expiry and R8’s after-pointer quoted-argument failure remain failed history; R9 fixes only strictly bounded fixture arguments, preserving the canonical general encoder. Consistent sample-service loading/error/freshness presentation remains open; notes already show acknowledged save state.</td><td>Pure lifecycle/storage policy, protocol adapters and typed client states. The <a href={sourceUrl('docs/native-notifications.md')}>notification guide</a> and <a href={sourceUrl('docs/notification-images.md')}>image guide</a> distinguish implemented storage/content from remaining acceptance.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>These are tracked requirements, not features implied by the screenshots. The <a href={sourceUrl('docs/implementation-status.json')}>implementation ledger</a> records acceptance criteria and evidence. Interactive sign-in with the rebuilt root greeter is now verified. Production authentication audits, native denied-lock recovery, authority/service faults, physical sessions and driver-fault injection remain open.</p>
