  <section id="next"><span class="eyebrow">07 / REFACTORING ORDER</span><h2>Use implemented use cases<br />to justify each abstraction.</h2>
    <ol class="guide-steps"><li>Use the established notification and native-control boundaries to make sample-service status cohesive. Preserve unavailable/error outcomes through ports and adapters instead of converting them to empty data.</li><li>Investigate active dock/widget buffer lifetimes and peak image preparation alongside prepared-pixel and texture counters. Keep client asset policy separate from generic renderer resources.</li><li>Keep renderer admission limits separate from memory budgets and native recovery policy. Extend layout and text contracts through concrete input use cases, without treating offscreen rejection tests as device-loss evidence.</li><li>Validate the implemented output identities, per-monitor profiles and fractional-density path through simultaneous native input, hotplug and suspend in a real compositor session.</li><li>Continue applying explicit inputs and typed intents where bar coordination warrants a separate owner. Strengthen domain values where invalid inputs reach persistence or external commands, preserving migration behavior.</li><li>Complete each retained capability through its port, adapter, usable client and reversible acceptance check. Keep native shell ownership and secure rollback intact.</li></ol>
    <p>For changes, use the repository's <a href={sourceUrl('.agents/skills/lucent-framework/SKILL.md')}>framework skill</a> or <a href={sourceUrl('.agents/skills/lucent-desktop/SKILL.md')}>desktop/integration skill</a>. The <a href={withBase('/docs/library/')}>library guide</a> and <a href={withBase('/docs/desktop/')}>user configuration guide</a> keep implementation concepts separate from everyday settings.</p>
